//// Front 20 F11 of 1.0.10-beta — `?T` has no `expect`. It existed, declared
//// `expect(self, default: T) -> T` and lowered to exactly `unwrapOr`, with the
//// prose saying so and the NAME saying the absent branch is unreachable. A
//// word that reads as an assertion over a signature that is a fallback is the
//// loosest reading of the stricter word, which is the shape decision 67
//// refuses; the strict reading (fail when absent) has to invent a failure the
//// option surface does not have, and belongs to whoever adds it on four
//// backends. So there is one spelling for one behaviour.
////
//// The refusal is explicit rather than left to the unknown-method fallback,
//// which types a method it does not know permissively: without it this program
//// would compile and fail at run time, which is worse than the alias was.

pub fn main() {
    val xs = [1, 2, 3];
    val v = xs.at(9).expect(0);
    @print(v);
}
